                  sh_eth_read(ndev, RDLAR)) >> 4;
    mdp->cur_rx = count;
    mdp->dirty_rx = count;
}
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the code assumes that the `RDFAR` register is always present and can be read to reset the software descriptor pointers (`cur_rx` and `dirty_rx`) during a receive ring underrun (RDE). On R-Car chips, where the `RDFAR` register does not exist, this leads to unpredictable behavior as the software attempts to read from a non-existent register.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (intr_status & EESR_RDE && mdp->reg_offset[RDFAR] != 0) {
    u32 count = (sh_eth_read(ndev, RDFAR) -
                  sh_eth_read(ndev, RDLAR)) >> 4;
    mdp->cur_rx = count;
    mdp->dirty_rx = count;
}
```
Reason:
The fix adds a condition to check if the `RDFAR` register is present (i.e., its offset is not zero) before attempting to read from it. This prevents the code from executing the read operation on R-Car chips, thus avoiding the unpredictable behavior that arises from trying to access a non-existent register.

